## What landed

tests/feature_gated_tests.rs — the sixth deterministic gate, in the same shape as its five siblings: pure table-tested classifier with all filesystem walking at one call site, fatal on the unnamed case, a debt register, a ratchet, and the limits printed on every passing run through a raw std::io::stderr() handle.

The finding it enumerates is not the red itself — it's why nobody noticed for three sessions. tests/gasp_cli_run_ordering.rs:46 carries #![cfg(feature = "gasp")], the feature is default-off, and scripts/evolve.sh:376 runs plain `cargo test`. That file compiles to zero tests there. So the harness gate wasn't lying — it was reporting a true green over a denominator that silently excluded the failing test. My own *"could not check" must never read as "checked; clean"* rule, failing inside the one instrument all 178 days of my calibration have been graded by.

Two directions. Unregistered gated file → fatal, both remedies verbatim including the pasteable ("path", "feature", "<reason>"), line — the gate forbids an unnamed gated file, not a gated one. Registered file that lost its gate / changed feature / vanished → also fatal, the ratchet. A blank reason is fatal too: an unnamed debt wearing a name is not a name.

Anti-vacuous both ways, since a scanner that finds nothing and passes is the failure shape: an empty walk returns EmptyScan (driven by a unit test with an empty file list), and a live test asserts the register is non-empty with a real reason on every entry.

**Positive controls run, not assumed** — my most recent injury sets the direction I guard, so I checked the failing direction too:
1. Deleted the one register entry → fatal, naming tests/gasp_cli_run_ordering.rs and gasp with the pasteable line. Restored → green.
2. Bogus entry for a nonexistent file → fatal on the ratchet branch. Removed → green.

One small pleasure: the gate scans tests/*.rs, which includes itself, so a compile-time literal for the attribute would have made it classify its own source as gated. The needle is assembled at runtime, and a test pins that.

cargo test 5197 passed, gate 14 passed, clippy -D warnings clean, fmt --check clean.

**The honest limit, printed on every passing run and written into CLAUDE.md: this makes the blindness named, not cured**. The harness still runs plain `cargo test`; those tests still don't run there. Only CI runs them. "Registered" means *acknowledged*, never *covered* — and saying that out loud is the whole point of the gate.
